@cc [owner:id13,label:architecture] standalone-poc
DFS code MUST NOT depend on Dust product services, authorization helpers, or relational databases. Shared implementation and RocksDB artifacts MUST remain beneath x/jd/dfs; the sibling SlateDB adapter and its experiment artifacts MUST remain beneath x/jd/dfs-slate. Cloud resources MUST belong to dust-dev.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;concurrency] ordered-publication
Version, namespace, and policy validation MUST be serialized with the corresponding atomic storage batch. Source-engine RocksDB publication MUST keep WAL enabled and sync disabled; SlateDB publication MUST keep WAL enabled and return without awaiting remote durability. Source persistence barriers MUST serialize with each other and capture a published prefix and its pending byte count while publication is excluded. Source WAL durability waits MUST release the publication lock. Successful persistence MUST confirm only the captured prefix and subtract only its pending bytes; later publications MUST remain accounted for. Failed persistence MUST NOT advance the confirmed prefix or clear pending bytes, and MUST stop subsequent mutations. Separate search-projection transactions MUST follow their own durable commit protocol and MUST NOT change source mutation acknowledgements.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;concurrency;testing] acyclic-rename-validation
Renames MUST reject self/descendant destinations, invalid replacement kinds, nonempty destination directories, and stale source or destination entry tokens without publishing namespace or journal changes. Concurrent opposing directory moves MUST preserve an acyclic namespace and permit at most one winner. Opt-in rename timing MUST preserve these checks and emit logs only after releasing the mutation lock; successful rename timing MUST report the number of source-subtree nodes visited, including zero when no traversal occurs.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;performance;concurrency] rename-depth-validation
Namespace operations MUST NOT reject an acyclic tree solely for its depth. Rename validation MUST NOT enumerate source descendants. Cross-parent moves MUST reject destinations within the source subtree by checking destination ancestry while validation and publication remain serialized. Ancestry consumers MUST detect cycles explicitly and retain authorization, replacement, retry, and entry-token checks.

@cc [owner:id13,label:security] current-authority
Every server operation MUST bind the authenticated tenant, principal, scope, expiry, and incarnation. Content identity and retained history MUST NOT bypass current authorization. Shared views MUST NOT disclose inaccessible ancestry or siblings.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;error-handling] recovery-and-retries
Recovery MUST preserve a consistent prefix across namespace, content, policy, request outcomes, and journal. Old sessions MUST fail after restart. Retained retries MUST match caller and payload; absent outcomes from old incarnations MUST NOT replay.

@cc [owner:id13,label:performance] bounded-resources
RPC sizes, outstanding requests, tenant admission, snapshots, and client caches MUST have configured bounds. Publication MUST stop on persistent storage failure or quota exhaustion.

@cc [owner:id13,label:performance;concurrency] owned-byte-reservations
Allocations admitted through the common byte budget MUST acquire their reservation before construction and retain it until their final owner releases the allocation. Byte-budget waits MUST have a deadline and a bounded waiter count. Failed admission or growth MUST preserve existing charges. Reservation growth MUST NOT wait while retaining a partial allocation; callers MUST reserve before growing or fail without growth. Dropping or shrinking a reservation MUST release the corresponding capacity and wake eligible waiters. Reservation metrics MUST distinguish retained charges, peak charges, waits, and rejected admissions from process RSS and native allocations.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;performance] rocksdb-cache-ownership
RocksDB instances sharing a StoreMemory owner MUST use its single block cache and write-buffer manager across all column families. Index/filter blocks and memtable reservations MUST participate in that cache budget. Reported memtable usage MUST NOT be added to cache usage as if it were an independent charge. Distinct StoreMemory owners MUST remain independently reclaimable. Cache capacity MUST NOT be represented as a strict process-memory bound. Write-buffer budgets below 1 MiB or above cache capacity MUST fail explicitly; memtable and arena sizing MUST scale with that budget so an initial allocation cannot stall a small valid configuration indefinitely.

@cc [owner:id13,label:testing] evidence-before-claims
Results MUST distinguish publication from persistence, observed process crashes from simulated power loss, and first invocation from controlled cold caches. Performance claims MUST be backed by raw data from correctness-valid runs.

@cc [owner:id13,label:security;concurrency] kernel-cache-coherence
In the legacy Watch-driven mount, cached kernel names, attributes, directory listings, and file contents MUST be invalidated after changes to their identity, metadata, visibility, authority, or server incarnation. A complete authorized-view refresh within one server incarnation MUST preserve cached content for unchanged readable files and invalidate changed authority, content, and namespace entries. The explicit kernel-writeback experiment MAY retire a remotely changed file generation as specified by experimental-kernel-writeback; default mounts MUST retain their existing live-handle refresh semantics. Known inodes absent from the refreshed view, including unlinked open files, MUST be invalidated because their authority cannot be established from that view. Credential loss and server incarnation changes MUST invalidate all known inodes. The refreshed view MUST NOT be reported as reconciled until required kernel invalidations complete. New opens after a server incarnation change MUST NOT refill an old handle's retired file inode. Namespace changes MUST invalidate directory pages for both old and new parents, including previously empty directories. Rewinding a directory handle after reconciliation MUST expose current authorized entries. Atomic replacement MUST NOT expose a transient absent path merely because the kernel references the replaced inode. Replies that populate these caches MUST serialize with view replacement and MUST NOT repopulate an obsolete content version. Kernel invalidation MUST run without holding locks needed by filesystem callbacks. Failure to invalidate MUST terminate the mount rather than silently retaining stale authority.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;error-handling] writable-close-errors
Skipping close-time flush callbacks MUST be limited to read-only handles. Writable handles MUST retain deferred publication-error checks, and explicit fsync MUST still validate the handle and prior publication outcome.

@cc [owner:id13,label:performance] demand-first-content
Default mount startup MUST fetch no file contents. Daemon content-cache residency MUST remain within the configured byte budget independently of workspace size. Kernel content caching MUST be demand-filled and reclaimable; sandbox deployments MUST bound total memory through a cgroup containing the application and mount daemon. Measurements MUST report that total memory limit and page-cache usage separately from daemon RSS and daemon cache bytes. Speculative reads MUST be triggered by actual access, bounded in bytes and concurrency, and MUST NOT recursively preload the workspace. Foreground FUSE dispatch MUST NOT wait for network content reads or adjacent-file prefetch. Speculative cache entries MUST NOT evict demand-cache entries. Reads spanning adjacent missing blocks SHOULD share a bounded demand RPC without fetching beyond the blocks intersecting the request.

@cc [owner:id13,label:security;concurrency] asynchronous-content-authority
Asynchronous content fetches MUST retain their requested node version and server incarnation. Results from an obsolete incarnation or authorization generation MUST NOT be admitted to the current cache or returned as successful reads. Cached content MUST NOT bypass revoked read permission; unlinked-handle reads MUST retain server-side authorization checks. Tokio async workers MUST NOT block waiting for a gate that a synchronous FUSE callback can hold across an RPC.

@cc [owner:id13,label:security;performance] selective-content-retention
In the legacy Watch-driven mount, a refreshed authorized view MAY retain daemon blocks only when the server incarnation, node identity, and version are unchanged and both views authorize READ. Retained blocks MUST preserve eviction order, speculative accounting, and the configured byte bounds. Namespace comparison MUST include both old and new projected parents and names. Authorization generation changes MUST continue to reject stale in-flight reads and speculative replies, even when unrelated resident content is retained.

@cc [owner:id13,label:security;concurrency] projected-entry-invalidation
Selective kernel invalidation batches MUST include at most one old and one new ViewNode per identity. A repeated identity/parent/name tuple MUST represent that entry existing in both views. Such unchanged names MUST retain their dentries while affected inode attributes or pages are invalidated. Added, removed, or moved entries MUST invalidate the applicable old/new names and parent directory pages. Broad resets MUST bypass name retention.

@cc [owner:id13,label:architecture;backend] interchangeable-store-boundary
The shared filesystem engine MUST access persistence, snapshots, batch encoding, and storage properties only through the store module. Switching storage engines MUST preserve the wire schema, tuple and value encoding, authorization, mutation ordering, client cache behavior, and logical quota accounting.

@cc [owner:id13,label:security] explicit-tls-provider
The server and client MUST initialize a Rustls crypto provider before configuring TLS, even when storage dependencies enable multiple providers. Initialization MUST preserve an already installed provider and otherwise select ring for both database engines.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;security] persisted-index-export
Index export MUST require a current unscoped administrator session. A lease MUST bind its owning session and tenant, expire within sixty seconds of creation or its last successful renewal and no later than session expiry, and expose only live metadata from a snapshot captured with its published prefix while publication is excluded. The captured prefix MUST be successfully persisted before the lease is returned; WAL durability waits MUST NOT exclude publication. Reads MUST use the immutable version captured by that lease, including after a later unlink. Version manifests and chunks referenced by unexpired leases MUST remain immutable and retained. Export MUST reject expired, foreign, or absent leases and bound active leases, metadata pages, journal ranges, and content reads. Ordinary Read authorization MUST remain unchanged.

@cc [owner:id13,label:security;performance] bounded-search-authority
Search grant lookup MUST bind the current session and read the subject/group reverse grant indexes without scanning tenant nodes. Exported inherited-grant labels MUST retain stable node/subject identities across revocation and restoration. Grant definitions and namespace invalidation boundaries MUST publish atomically with their mutations. Privileged grant export MUST use the same persisted lease as node export. Search candidate validation MUST use one source snapshot, preserve namespace projection and current permissions, and reject more than one hundred candidates. New RPC variants MUST be appended without changing existing wire tags.

@cc [owner:id13,label:security;performance] indexed-namespace-deltas
Search context MUST derive current grants and namespace invalidations from one source snapshot. Each rename or unlink MUST atomically journal opaque labels for the moved/deleted subtree and any replaced destination. Context lookup MUST traverse at most 256 namespace events since the indexed boundary without enumerating tenant nodes, and MUST report incomplete coverage when the journal cannot cover that boundary. Labels MUST be bound to tenant and node identity and MUST NOT expose canonical ancestor IDs. Source incarnation or tenant mismatches MUST reject the context request.

@cc [owner:id13,label:security;performance] request-local-search-permissions
Candidate validation MUST resolve caller subjects once and reuse loaded nodes and inherited permissions for shared ancestors within its single source snapshot. Cached effective permissions MUST retain scope membership and cycle rejection. Permission state MUST NOT survive the validation request or bypass fresh revocation checks.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;performance;security] incremental-search-export
Incremental index export MUST retain persisted-index-export lease and snapshot guarantees. Within retained journal bounds, content mutations MUST export only changed node identities, including tombstones. Policy-only changes MUST NOT enumerate nodes or grants. Namespace resets and journal gaps MUST reconcile a complete live snapshot; this must include removed replacement targets. The legacy full export and wire discriminants MUST remain unchanged.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;security;concurrency] renewable-index-export
Renewal MUST require the current owning unscoped administrator session and an unexpired lease. It MUST extend only the deadline, preserving the captured tenant, prefix, node versions, and grants without opening a new snapshot. Expired, ended, logged-out, and foreign leases MUST NOT be revived. Indexers MUST stop renewal and release their lease after export or failure; abandonment MUST expire within sixty seconds of the last renewal. Failed renewal MUST prevent publication of the attempted search generation. Existing RPC discriminants MUST remain unchanged.

@cc [owner:id13,label:performance;concurrency] independent-read-admission
Demand RPC sizes and speculative fetch byte limits MUST remain independent of retained content capacity, including a zero-byte cache. A demand response MUST remain available to its requesting read until consumed without depending on cache admission. Demand RPC concurrency, in-flight response bytes, and pending FUSE reads MUST have independent positive bounds. Speculation MUST use separate admission and MUST NOT consume demand permits. In-flight response reuse MUST NOT be reported as a retained-cache hit. RPC reservations MUST charge transient decode/chunk-copy overlap. Read assembly and completed output MUST retain a separate byte reservation until the consumer finishes the FUSE reply; cancellation and error paths MUST release it. Consumers MUST consume or drop held replies before waiting for more output than the configured reply allowance.

@cc [owner:id13,label:performance;security;concurrency] isolated-view-streams
Snapshot and watch streams MUST have separate positive global and tenant concurrency limits. Tenant admission MUST bind the authenticated tenant across sessions and remain held while snapshot materialization or retained streaming work remains active. Stream overload MUST NOT consume unary RPC admission. Snapshot transmission MUST move nodes from one retained view into bounded chunks without constructing a second full-view chunk vector. Disconnects and errors MUST release stream admission; idle tenant admission entries MUST be reclaimable. Every snapshot part MUST retain the existing current-session and authorization-generation checks.

@cc [owner:id13,label:performance;concurrency] inode-lifetimes
Mount inode records MUST have a configured count bound and monotonically allocated inode numbers that are never reused. Successful entry, create, and non-dot readdirplus replies MUST retain kernel lookup references until forget. Open file and directory handles, retained directory snapshots, asynchronous reads, and pending notifications MUST pin their inode records until completion or disposal. Forgotten inodes MUST be reclaimed after their final reference ends, including on error paths. Retiring an active DFS identity MUST preserve older referenced inode records without letting their later reclamation remove a newer mapping. Notification targets MUST remain pinned while kernel notifications execute outside callback locks. Directory byte accounting MUST include retained inode pins.

@cc [owner:id13,label:security;backend] publication-view-pins
Authorized mutation outcomes and resolved publication outcomes that expose a node MUST register that identity in the receiving session's view pins, including successful retries. View pins MUST remain session-bound and MUST NOT grant permissions. The retained-node quota MUST count tombstones without refund on unlink, bounding each session's pin set by the tenant node high-water count. Logout and expired-session cleanup MUST reclaim pins; delayed view completion MUST NOT recreate pins for a removed or expired session. Current authority remains mandatory: every server read and stat through a pin MUST enforce current authority, including after unlink or revocation. Unlinked-file getattr without a supplied FUSE handle MAY use a current open server handle for the same retained inode; it MUST NOT use another inode generation's handle.

@cc [owner:id13,label:security;concurrency;performance] ordered-kernel-prefetch
Kernel prefetch insertion MUST serialize its validation and store notifications against local mutation and authorized-view replacement. It MUST release callback-state and reply-gate locks before executing store notifications, while allowing demand callbacks and read-only opens to make progress. Mutations awaiting that ordering MUST use a bounded queue and bounded retained write bytes outside the FUSE dispatch thread. Reconciliation MUST mark pending invalidation before releasing transition exclusion; it MUST execute notifications without blocking mutation callbacks and forbid kernel stores until notification completion. Failed invalidation MUST leave insertion disabled and terminate the mount. Insertion MUST validate incarnation, authority generation, READ permission, content version, and exact range length within verified EOF; it MUST exclude unresolved outcomes and writable or failed handles. Targets MUST retain inode pins throughout insertion. Store failure MUST invalidate its possibly inserted range before allowing further insertion, or terminate the mount. A panic during insertion MUST disable further insertion before releasing transition ordering and signal fatal mount shutdown. Notification success MUST NOT be reported as proven page residency. Speculative submission history MUST remain bounded independently of retained content capacity, and unsupported targets MAY use the existing bounded fallback cache.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;security;concurrency] publication-receipts
A publication receipt MUST bind the tenant, authenticated principal's request key, complete request identity, payload digest, and tenant outcome head to records in the same atomic batch as the mutation. Resolving an absent outcome MUST NOT publish a replacement request or claim that the request cannot still complete. Receipt resolution and persistence MUST enforce current authority. Recovery MUST verify the exact retained publication before confirming it; a recovered or later prefix with the same numerical head is insufficient. Legacy request outcomes and serialized RPC tags MUST remain readable without reinterpretation.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;error-handling;concurrency] durable-synchronization
Default mounted fsync MUST resolve preceding ambiguous publications and report publication errors without requesting server persistence. Storage persistence MUST require the explicit --durable-sync opt-in and confirm verified publication receipts. Publication-only mounts MUST NOT retain successful receipts solely for durability tracking. File synchronization MUST cover prior local publications for that inode; directory synchronization MUST cover prior local namespace publications affecting that directory. The legacy --publication-only-sync flag MUST remain accepted and MUST conflict with --durable-sync. Tenant heads MUST NOT be compared to engine persistence counters. Persistence waiters MUST release the mutation lock while storage flushes, and MAY share a flush covering their captured targets. Unsupported quorum durability MUST fail. Flush MUST report retained failures without requiring persistence. Unknown mutations MUST retain their identities and stop dependent publication; receipt recovery MUST invalidate affected kernel state outside callback locks before synchronization reports success. A failed recovery invalidation MUST terminate the mount.

@cc [owner:id13,label:architecture;performance] namespace-content-ownership
The mount MUST keep namespace identity/authority/progress and retained content in separate owned structures. It MUST NOT retain a second full snapshot node vector after namespace construction. Prefetch planning MUST use the current namespace after updates and removals. Namespace nodes and charged bytes MUST have independent configured limits. Oversized full snapshots and deltas MUST fail before changing the current namespace, cursor, or retained content; delta validation MUST include the entire batch. Local create and rename MUST reserve namespace capacity before publication. Reconciliation that cannot fit the current scope MUST terminate the mount instead of indefinitely serving its previous view.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;performance;security] bounded-view-working-state
Server view construction MUST visit stored rows incrementally and stop when its configured node or charged working-byte allowance is exhausted. The allowance MUST include nodes, groups, grants, projection/index overhead, and temporary ancestry work. Permission and depth results MUST be shared across ancestry walks; the server MUST NOT retain an ancestry vector for every node. Cycle, scope, inheritance, and projected-name checks MUST remain intact. A failed snapshot MUST release stream admission without blocking ordinary point operations. Byte charges MUST be described as managed allowances rather than total process RSS limits.

@cc [owner:id13,label:security;backend;concurrency] fenced-writer-generations
Experimental writeback handles MUST bind an authenticated session, tenant, node, and nonreused exclusive-writer generation. Acquisition, renewal, final release, and mutation-time generation checks MUST serialize with publication. New content or attribute publication through an expired, replaced, absent, or foreign fenced handle MUST fail; ordinary writers MUST NOT bypass an active fence. Fenced publication and renewal MUST require current READ and WRITE permission. Retrying a published request MAY resolve its recorded outcome after ownership loss but MUST NOT publish again. Closing an older generation MUST NOT release its replacement. Restart MUST invalidate old sessions/handles. Active-open retries MUST reuse their handle without consuming capacity; new handles and ownership generations MUST remain bounded by server handle admission. Logout and export MUST preserve the index-lease-before-publication lock order.

@cc [owner:id13,label:security;concurrency;error-handling] experimental-kernel-writeback
Kernel writeback MUST require explicit opt-in and negotiated kernel support. Buffered writable handles MUST acquire server-fenced READ and WRITE ownership; WRITE-only handles MUST retain direct I/O. Handles of one owned inode MUST share a publication sequence and expected server version. Buffered append MUST publish the kernel-provided offset without appending a complete page again. Ownership MUST remain renewable while kernel file references or writable mappings retain a handle. Lease loss, rejected batches, uncertain publication, and write-admission failures MUST stop dependent publication and retain an inode failure across synchronization, close, and new opens. Exact receipt resolution MUST NOT clear that failure or rebase remaining dirty pages. Failed inodes MUST remain bounded by writer admission and require a fresh mount for recovery. Potentially dirty and failed writer inodes MUST be excluded from kernel prefetch insertion. Kernel writeback requests MUST have reserved queue and payload admission independent of ordinary mutations/direct writes. Negotiated kernel background admission MUST fit that reserve, including one replied request still completing userspace cleanup, so dirty-page pressure throttles without rejecting already accepted writes for local capacity. A partial-page fill racing another local batch MAY retry only against the same fenced writer's advanced version with unchanged incarnation and authority; it MUST NOT advance the writer's expected version. Read completion MUST hold the reply gate without blocking an async executor. Because Linux globally trusts writeback inode size and timestamps, remotely changed file versions in the explicit experiment MUST retire their old kernel inode under the reconciliation gate. Invalidation MUST retain and invalidate the retired inode and force name relookup even for unchanged paths. The daemon MUST reject retired handles with ESTALE (Linux cached page reads MAY surface that error as EIO); reopening MUST allocate a fresh inode with current attributes. Unlink without replacement of that identity MUST preserve the normal authorized open-handle path. Local rename outcomes MUST NOT silently advance a writer base or hide a remote content change from reconciliation. The legacy mount's default direct-write semantics MUST remain unchanged; the default live mount follows its buffered-publication contract.

@cc [owner:id13,label:testing;concurrency] concurrent-namespace-oracles
The namespace race benchmark MUST use independent client sessions and force both serial orders in addition to concurrent trials. It MUST validate allowed errors, publication order, unchanged heads on failed mutations, exact final namespace/content, replacement identities, and retained-handle identity. Error and success latency MUST be reported separately; a failed oracle MUST fail the run and preserve its record. RPC race evidence MUST NOT be presented as cross-mount cache, distributed failover, or cross-shard transaction validation.

@cc [owner:id13,label:security;cli] default-one-second-mount
The dfs-mount executable MUST use the one-second metadata/revision and optimistic-publication implementation. The Watch/kernel-data-cache implementation MUST require the explicit dfs-mount-legacy executable. Compatibility aliases MUST NOT select weaker consistency or publication-only fsync. AllowOther MUST enable kernel permission checking with the configured owner identity in addition to server authorization.


@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;concurrency;error-handling] bounded-buffered-file-publication
Default mounts MAY acknowledge bounded file creation, content and attribute changes before remote publication. Pending bytes MUST remain mount-owned across close, with at most 128 files or directories and two MiB of file payload in a publication batch. Background publication MUST become eligible within 100 ms of the oldest remaining record's original acceptance and apply backpressure while publishing. Edits and retries MUST NOT reset acceptance times; a partial drain MUST remove timestamps only for confirmed records. Explicit fsync and graceful unmount MUST drain accepted changes and confirm durability; failures MUST propagate. Definite rejection MUST retain a deferred failure, and ambiguous retries MUST reuse the sealed identity and payload. Capacity-triggered publication MAY exclude the currently edited file, but the selected subset MUST remain sealed across uncertain retries and synchronization MUST drain both selected and retained files. A batch MUST validate current authority, absent names and identities for creates, and original revision and entry identity for replacements atomically with every file, journal event, index event and receipt. New directories MUST precede their descendants in a sealed batch and inherit only validated parent authority. Directory attribute replacements MUST retain the original revision and entry identity, and directory records MUST carry no file payload. Publication MUST NOT advance a client's contiguous journal cursor. Client metadata and kernel TTLs MUST expire within 500 ms from validation start; cache hits MUST NOT renew authority. Delayed or partitioned publication MUST NOT be described as satisfying a one-second visibility bound from local acknowledgement.

@cc [owner:id13,label:backend;performance;concurrency] bounded-incremental-refresh
A delta within 4096 retained journal events MUST remain incremental unless an authority/reset event requires a new view. Distributed implementations MUST read the contiguous journal range, verify sequence identity, and fetch changed nodes and retained pins in bounded batches. A journal gap or expired source snapshot MUST fail rather than skip events. A local publication receipt MUST NOT alone advance the client's contiguous journal cursor.
